On Feb 21, 2:54 am, "slayer88" <slayergwarironmai...@yahoo.com> wrote:
> I'm new to nethack and I am addicted to it. Does anyone know what the
> all around best class is?

As others have pointed out, there isn't a single best class, however,
here are some of my observations:

Valkyries are probably the easiest melee class, with Samurai being a
close second. Samurai have to be lawful and have a prohibition against
attacking peacefuls, which is a minor problem, and they can't get to
expert in daggers, but they can in bows, and start with both strong
ranged and melee attacks. They also (usually) have the strongest
starting armor of any class. On the downside, splint mail is heavy.
Both Valks and Sams have a good first sacrifice gift (Mjollnir and
Snickersnee respectively), and can get Excalibur (Lawful Valks only,
though), which makes weapons not such a problem, and they can both
#twoweapon, though Samurai are a bit better at it. Barbarians are
pretty strong too, and start with poison resistance, which is nice.
Knights are a bit of a mixed bag, being much more difficult early on
than you'd expect given their starting gear, but can be very powerful
with practice. They jump, and start with a lance, which are both very
useful if you know how to use them.

Wizards, while a bit unconventional, can start with very nice starting
kit, and force bolt is useful for anyone. A Wizard who starts with a
good set of rings, and/or the spell of ID or sleep might well have an
easy time of it. Healers, Monks and Priests are the next-best casters
all around, but both Healers and Monks are very tricky. Priests know
the cursedness of any item, which is a dangerously addictive ability,
and may well save you - but beware their disappointingly weak weapon
choices.

Rogues and Rangers excel at ranged combat, and Rogues can be melee
powerhouses, too - but they lack HP.

Tourists and Archaeologists excel at flexibility. Tourists,
especially, can take advantage of almost anything the RNG throws at
them, but both have mediocre-at-best starting stats and equipment,
which tends to make life difficult.

I'd personally recommend starting as a Valkyrie or a Samurai, though
if you're feeling unconventional Wizards, Rogues and Knights can be
interesting starters, too. The first time I ever ascended in 1.3.x
(Don't recall the exact version, but suffice it to say that Elf was a
class back then, Gehennom was Hell and you died instantly there
without FR, and there was no Quest, Mines or Sokoban!) was with a
Knight. (Though I'm still working on ascending in 3.4.3, having missed
out on everything between 1.3.x and 3.4.1 except for an unknown
version of Slash'EM in between)



